            "**Write the epic statement.** Use an outcome, story-style or hypothesis format to say who benefits and why.",
            "**Define success.** Choose one to three measures with a baseline and a target, such as 'return contacts fall from about 2,400 to under 1,000 a month within three months of launch'.",
            "**Set boundaries.** List what is in and out of scope, and anything still undecided.",
            "**Add high-level acceptance criteria.** Three to six conditions that describe the finished outcome.",
            "**Note dependencies and risks.** Identify other teams, suppliers, data or approvals you need, and plan early stories or spikes to reduce the biggest risk.",
            "**Size it roughly.** Ask the team for a T-shirt size or a story-point range. If it looks larger than a quarter, split it into two epics with separate goals.",
            "**Split the first slice into stories.** Write the stories for a thin end-to-end slice first and leave later parts rough until you get closer. Our [user story template](/templates/user-story-template/) includes an epic sheet.",
            "**Order it in the backlog.** Place the epic in the [Product Backlog](/project-management/scrum/product-backlog/) by value, risk and dependencies, and review it in [backlog refinement](/project-management/scrum/backlog-refinement/). Our [product backlog template](/templates/product-backlog-template/) keeps the order, value and estimates in one place.",
